Requirements Driver framework 
● Implement a Linux I2C driver that binds to the ENV-COMBO device. 
● Exercise judgement as to how to map the registers.
● Integrate with the IIO (Industrial IO) subsystem and implement IIO_TEMP and IIO_HUMIDITYRELATIVE channels, 
and implement a triggered buffer setup. ● At this stage, there is no need to consider the ALS functionality.

1. Set kernel environment: source env
2. build driver: make
3. move drivers (cs-driver.ko, combo_sensor_driver) to target: scp ...
3. run bash: ./test.sh

IIO_TEMP is defined in the Linux kernel source in the Industrial I/O (IIO) subsystem headers. Specifically, it is defined in:
include/linux/iio/types.h
Here’s a snippet from that file:
enum iio_chan_type {
    IIO_VOLTAGE,
    IIO_CURRENT,
    IIO_POWER,
    IIO_ACCEL,
    IIO_ANGL_VEL,
    ...
    IIO_TEMP,
    IIO_HUMIDITYRELATIVE,
    ...
};
Binding Mechanism

1. i2c_device_id Table
static const struct i2c_device_id env_combo_id[] = {
    { "combo-sensor", 0 },
    { }
};
MODULE_DEVICE_TABLE(i2c, env_combo_id);

This table associates the driver with devices whose I2C name matches "combo-sensor".
MODULE_DEVICE_TABLE() ensures this table is available to userspace tools (like udev).

2. i2c_driver Structure
static struct i2c_driver env_combo_driver = {
    .driver = {
        .name = "combo-driver",
    },
    .probe = env_combo_probe,
    .id_table = env_combo_id,
};
module_i2c_driver(env_combo_driver);
This tells the kernel: “If an I2C device with name combo-sensor is registered, call env_combo_probe().”

3. Device Tree (if applicable) or Board File
To actually match, a device with name "env-combo" must exist in the system. This typically happens through:

A Device Tree entry (if using DT):
i2c@1 {
    env_combo@39 {
        compatible = "vendor,env-combo";
        reg = <0x39>;
    };
};


When Exactly Is env_combo_read_raw() Called?

It is triggered when userspace accesses:

/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/in_temp_raw
/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/in_temp_scale
/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/in_humidityrelative_raw
/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/in_humidityrelative_scale


For example:
cat /sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:device0/in_temp_raw
This calls:
→ IIO core .read_raw = env_combo_read_raw()

Roles of Each Module
Module	Role
i2c-envcombo-sim.ko	Simulates an I2C device at a known address (0x39), responding like the ENV-COMBO sensor.
cs_combo.ko	 Kernel I2C client driver that detects and communicates with the simulated device.

Diagram:
Userspace (e.g. cat /sys/bus/iio/devices/.../in_temp_raw)
       ↓
IIO subsystem
       ↓
cs-driver.ko (client driver)
       ↓
I2C core
       ↓
i2c-envcombo-sim.ko (simulated device)

Use dmesg to verify it loaded, and ls /sys/bus/iio/devices/ to inspect sysfs entries.

>dmesg | tail -f

Test on QEMU (for RPI3+ )
==================================================================================================================================
Building a QEMU Environment with the Yocto Project

The Yocto Project is a powerful collaboration that helps you create custom Linux-based systems for embedded devices, including a build-to-run QEMU environment. This guide will walk you through the process of setting up and building a QEMU image from scratch.

Step 1: Install Host Prerequisites
Before you begin, ensure your host machine has the necessary packages. This example uses a Debian/Ubuntu system.

sudo apt-get update
sudo apt-get install gawk wget git diffstat unzip texinfo gcc build-essential chrpath socat
sudo apt-get install python3 python3-pip python3-pexpect xz-utils debianutils iproute2
sudo apt-get install libtool zlib1g-dev libncurses5-dev libssl-dev

Step 2: Set up the Yocto Build Environment
Clone the Poky reference distribution and initialize the build environment. This process creates a build directory where all your work will take place.

# Clone the Poky reference distribution
git clone git://git.yoctoproject.org/poky.git
cd poky
git checkout yocto-4.0.1 # Use a stable release

# Source the environment script to set up your shell
source oe-init-build-env

Step 3: Add Necessary Layers
For a Raspberry Pi build, you need to add the meta-raspberrypi layer. The meta-qemu layer is part of Poky, so it's included by default.

# From inside the 'build' directory
bitbake-layers add-layer ../meta-raspberrypi

Step 4: Configure the local.conf File
This is the most crucial step. You must edit the conf/local.conf file to configure your build.

Open the file and add or modify the following lines:

# In conf/local.conf
# This line defines the target machine.
# To build a generic ARM QEMU image:
#MACHINE = "qemuarm64"
# Or, to specifically build for the Raspberry Pi 3:
MACHINE = "raspberrypi3"

# This is a good starting point for a minimal filesystem with networking
IMAGE_FEATURES += "ssh-server-openssh"
IMAGE_INSTALL += "packagegroup-core-boot packagegroup-core-full-cmdline"

# Specify the kernel type
PREFERRED_PROVIDER_virtual/kernel = "linux-raspberrypi"

# Enable debugging if needed
EXTRA_IMAGE_FEATURES = "debug-tweaks"

Step 5: Start the Build
Now, you are ready to start the build. Yocto will download all the sources, compile the toolchain, the kernel, and the root filesystem. This process will take a considerable amount of time.

# This command builds the core-image-full-cmdline image for your machine
bitbake core-image-full-cmdline

But the most important one for booting is the .qcow2 image. You can use the runqemu script provided by Yocto to start the emulation.

# Navigate to the images directory
cd tmp/deploy/images/raspberrypi3/

# Use the Yocto helper script to run QEMU
runqemu raspberrypi3

This guide should help you build a complete, self-contained QEMU environment for your kernel driver development.
======================================================================================================================================================
Builiding sdk populate
bitbake core-image-minimal -c populate-sdk

After a successful populate-sdk, the SDK is typically placed in:
<YOCTO_BUILD_DIR>/tmp/deploy/sdk/

To install the SDK:
cd ~/yocto/build/tmp/deploy/sdk
chmod +x ./oecore-x86_64-core-image-minimal-aarch64-toolchain-<timestamp>.sh
./oecore-x86_64-core-image-minimal-aarch64-toolchain-<timestamp>.sh

It will install into a directory like /opt/... and provide an environment-setup-* script. Source it before building your kernel module:
source /opt/poky/.../environment-setup-aarch64-poky-linux

To confirm what compiler is used on your system
cat /lib/modules/$(uname -r)/build/Makefile | grep '^CC'

1. Create a Makefile in the same directory as your cs_driver.c:
# Makefile for env_combo I2C driver

obj-m += cs_driver.o	

KDIR := /lib/modules/$(shell uname -r)/build
PWD := $(shell pwd)

all:
	$(MAKE) -C $(KDIR) M=$(PWD) modules

clean:
	$(MAKE) -C $(KDIR) M=$(PWD) clean

